'''John Edward James''' (born June 8, 1981<ref>{{cite web |title = James, John |url = https://bioguide.congress.gov/search/bio/J000307 |website = Biographical Directory of the United States Congress |access-date = October 21, 2024 }}</ref>) is an American politician, businessman, and former military officer serving as the [[United States House of Representatives|U.S. representative]] for [[Michigan's 10th congressional district]] since 2023.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Spangler |first1=Todd |date=January 31, 2022 |title=John James enters race for Congress after 2 unsuccessful runs for Senate |url=https://www.freep.com/story/news/politics/elections/2022/01/31/john-james-congress/9282514002/ |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221110162034/https://www.freep.com/story/news/politics/elections/2022/01/31/john-james-congress/9282514002/ |archive-date=November 10, 2022 |access-date=May 10, 2026 |work=[[Detroit Free Press]]}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |date = November 8, 2022 |title = Michigan 10th Congressional District Election Results |language = en-US |work = [[The New York Times]] |url = https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2022/11/08/us/elections/results-michigan-us-house-district-10.html |access-date = November 9, 2022 |issn = 0362-4331 }}</ref> A member of the [[Republican Party (United States)|Republican Party]], he was the party's nominee for the [[United States Senate|U.S. Senate]] in [[2018 United States Senate election in Michigan|2018]] and [[2020 United States Senate election in Michigan|2020]].<ref name="politicopeterswins">{{Cite news |url = https://www.politico.com/news/2020/11/04/michigan-senate-election-results-2020-433579 |title = Gary Peters Wins Reelection in Michigan |first = Brianna |last = Ehley |date = November 4, 2020 |website = [[Politico]] }}</ref>

In [[2022 United States House of Representatives elections in Michigan#District 10|2022]], James declared his candidacy in Michigan's redrawn 10th congressional district, a seat he won and was re-elected to in [[2024 United States House of Representatives elections in Michigan#District 10|2024]].

James is the [[Michigan Republican Party|Republican]] nominee in the [[2026 Michigan gubernatorial election]] and faces Secretary of State [[Jocelyn Benson]] in the general.

==Early life and military career==
James was born in [[Southfield, Michigan]], in 1981 and grew up Baptist<ref name=":4">{{cite news |last = Miller |first = John J. |date = May 14, 2020 |title = John James, the Michigan GOP's Rising Star |url = https://www.nationalreview.com/magazine/2020/06/01/john-james-the-michigan-gops-rising-star/ |access-date = November 6, 2020 |website = National Review |language = en-US }}</ref> in the [[Palmer Woods]] neighborhood of [[Detroit]]. He graduated from the Catholic [[Brother Rice High School (Michigan)|Brother Rice High School]] in 1999.<ref name=Peters>{{cite news |first = Jeremy W. |last = Peters |url = https://www.nytimes.com/2018/08/03/us/politics/john-james-michigan.html |title = John James, Black and Republican, Thinks He Can Crack the 'Blue Wall' in Michigan |work = [[The New York Times]] |date = August 3, 2018 }}</ref> He graduated from the [[United States Military Academy]] (West Point) in 2004,<ref name=Peters/><ref name="Livengood">{{cite news |first = Chad |last = Livengood |url = https://www.crainsdetroit.com/article/20170718/news/634161/detroit-businessman-john-e-james-moves-into-campaign-mode-for-us-senate |title = Detroit Businessman John E. James Moves into Campaign Mode for U.S. Senate |work = Crain's Detroit Business |date = July 18, 2017 }}</ref> and served eight years in the [[United States Army|Army]], participating in multiple tours of duty in [[Operation Iraqi Freedom]] as an [[Boeing AH-64 Apache|AH-64 Apache]] pilot.<ref name=":2">{{cite news |first1 = R.J. |last1 = King |first2 = John S. |last2 = Schultz |first3 = Tom |last3 = Beaman |first4 = Tim |last4 = Keenan |first5 = Dan |last5 = Calabrese |name-list-style = amp |date = October 8, 2012 |url = http://www.dbusiness.com/September-October-2012/30-in-Their-Thirties/#.W8-vVhNKi8U |title = 30 in Their Thirties 2012 |work = Detroit Business Journal |access-date = October 28, 2018 }}</ref><ref>{{cite news |first = Jenni |last = Fink |date = October 9, 2018 |url = https://www.newsweek.com/trump-jr-embodies-party-privilege-activist-says-michigan-gop-senate-candidate-1160709 |title = Trump Jr. Embodies Party of Privilege, Activist Says, But Michigan GOP Senate Candidate Says His Ancestors Were Slaves |work = [[Newsweek]] |access-date = October 28, 2018 }}</ref> Two of his West Point classmates are fellow congressmen [[Wesley Hunt]] and [[Pat Ryan (politician)|Pat Ryan]].<ref>{{Cite web |date=November 26, 2022 |title=Bipartisan band of brothers: The West Point grads coming to Congress |url=https://www.politico.com/news/2022/11/26/west-point-grads-congress-midterm-results-00070710 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230207225005/https://www.politico.com/news/2022/11/26/west-point-grads-congress-midterm-results-00070710 |archive-date=February 7, 2023 |access-date=May 10, 2026 |website=[[Politico]] |language=en}}</ref> He attended [[Ranger School]] and became Ranger-tabbed as a captain.<ref>{{cite web |url = https://www.mlive.com/news/2017/07/detroit_businessman_john_e_jam.html |title = Detroit Businessman John E. James Exploring Run for U.S. Senate |first = Lauren |last = Gibbons |work = [[MLive]] |publisher = Booth Michigan |date = July 18, 2017 |access-date = September 3, 2020 }}</ref><ref>{{cite press release |url = https://www.michigan.gov/dmva/0,9665,7-402--323271--,00.html |title = John James to Lead Michigan Veterans Trust Fund Board |first = Veronica |last = LaDuke |publisher = [[Michigan Department of Military and Veterans Affairs]] |date = March 5, 2014 |access-date = September 3, 2020 }}</ref>

James received a master's degree in [[supply chain management]] from [[Penn State University]]'s [[Smeal College of Business]]<ref name=":2" /> and an [[MBA]] from the [[University of Michigan]]'s [[Ross School of Business]].<ref name=":3">{{cite news |last=Johnstone |first=Keith |date=September 21, 2020 |title=As a Black man, I Am Ashamed of John James |url=https://www.michigandaily.com/section/columns/black-man-i-am-ashamed-john-james |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201031202818/https://www.michigandaily.com/section/columns/black-man-i-am-ashamed-john-james |archive-date=October 31, 2020 |access-date=May 10, 2026 |website=The Michigan Daily |location=University of Michigan |language=en |type=Opinion}}</ref>

==Business career==
In 2012, James joined James Group International, where his father, John A. James, was the CEO.<ref>{{cite news |last=Karoub |first=Jeff |date=August 8, 2018 |title=Trump-Backed John James Wins Michigan's GOP Senate Nod |url=https://apnews.com/12bf6ba4d115405c9c57059c6873fd7c |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230307140228/https://apnews.com/article/12bf6ba4d115405c9c57059c6873fd7c |archive-date=March 7, 2023 |access-date=May 10, 2026 |publisher=[[Associated Press]]}}</ref> James Group is a global [[supply chain]] management service company; James became its director of operations, and eventually became president of James Group International and CEO of its subsidiary, Renaissance Global Logistics.<ref>{{cite news |first = Chad |last = Livengood |url = https://www.crainsdetroit.com/article/20180415/news/658191/from-a-liquidated-trucking-company-a-logistics-group-rises |title = From a Liquidated Trucking Company, a Logistics Group Rises |work = Crain's Detroit Business |date = April 15, 2018 }}</ref><ref name="Livengood" /> Renaissance Global, based in Detroit, was the recipient of a $1–2 million [[Paycheck Protection Program]] loan during the [[COVID-19 pandemic in Michigan|COVID-19 pandemic]].<ref>{{cite news |last1=Eggert |first1=David |date=July 6, 2020 |title=Firms tied to legislative leader, Senate Candidate Got Loans |url=https://apnews.com/bc62d7f2c257f4b5b27840dc51319953 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230216065920/https://apnews.com/bc62d7f2c257f4b5b27840dc51319953 |archive-date=February 16, 2023 |access-date=May 10, 2026 |publisher=[[Associated Press]]}}</ref>

James was named one of ''Detroit Business Journal's'' 30 in their 30s of 2012, and ''[[Michigan Chronicle]]''<nowiki/>'s 40 under 40 of 2014.<ref>{{cite news |first = Tatiana |last = Wheeler |date = October 1, 2014 |url = https://michronicleonline.com/2014/10/01/congratulations-to-our-class-of-2014-40-under-40/ |title = Congratulations to Our Class of 2014 40 Under 40! |work = [[Michigan Chronicle]] |access-date = October 28, 2018 |archive-date = February 7, 2019 |archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20190207215940/https://michronicleonline.com/2014/10/01/congratulations-to-our-class-of-2014-40-under-40/ |url-status = dead }}</ref> He served as a board member of the Michigan Council for Future Mobility, Michigan Minority Supplier Development Council and National Veteran Business Development Council. He serves on the Detroit Workforce Development Board.<ref>{{cite news |last=McKinney |first=Jeffrey |date=June 7, 2017 |title=Is This BE 100s Executive Michigan's Next Republican Senator? |url=https://www.blackenterprise.com/be-100s-executive-michigans-next-republican-senator/ |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260217024749/https://www.blackenterprise.com/be-100s-executive-michigans-next-republican-senator/ |archive-date=February 17, 2026 |access-date=May 10, 2026 |work=[[Black Enterprise]]}}</ref>

==Political career==
===2018 U.S. Senate race===
{{Main|2018 United States Senate election in Michigan}}
[[File:Donald Trump, with John Edward James, Oval Office (September 2018).jpg|left|thumb|James with Donald Trump at the [[White&nbsp;House]] in September 2018]]
In September 2017, James entered the [[Republican Party (United States)|Republican]] primary for the [[2018 United States Senate election in Michigan]]<ref>{{cite news |url = https://www.freep.com/story/news/local/michigan/2017/09/21/john-james-u-s-senate-debbie-stabenow/689425001/ |title = Metro Detroit Businessman John James Joins U.S. Senate Race |first = Todd |last = Spangler |work = [[Detroit Free Press]] |date = September 21, 2017 |access-date = November 26, 2018 }}</ref> in an attempt to unseat three-term incumbent [[Democratic Party (United States)|Democrat]] [[Debbie Stabenow]], as well as become Michigan's first African-American senator. Despite musician and Michigan native [[Kid Rock]] publicly toying with the idea of running for the seat for months, the primary came down to James and [[Grosse Pointe]] businessman and former Wayne County commissioner Sandy Pensler.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Oosting |first1=Jonathan |last2=Nann Burke |first2=Melissa |name-list-style=amp |date=November 20, 2017 |title=Pensler Joins U.S. Senate Race to Take on Stabenow |url=https://www.detroitnews.com/story/news/politics/2017/11/20/sandy-pensler-senate-campaign/107860320/ |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260420020144/https://www.detroitnews.com/story/news/politics/2017/11/20/sandy-pensler-senate-campaign/107860320/ |archive-date=April 20, 2026 |access-date=May 10, 2026 |work=[[The Detroit News]]}}</ref> James was endorsed via [[Twitter]] by [[President of the United States|President]] [[Donald Trump]] on July 27, 2018, eleven days before the primary.<ref>{{cite news |url = https://www.mlive.com/news/index.ssf/2018/07/donald_trump_endorses_john_jam.html |title = Donald Trump Endorses John James in Michigan's U.S. Senate Race |first = Lauren |last = Gibbons |work = [[MLive]] |publisher = Booth Michigan |date = July 27, 2018 |access-date = November 26, 2018 }}</ref> James won the nomination with 55% of the vote.<ref>{{cite web |author = Bureau of Elections |url = https://mielections.us/election/results/2018PRI_CENR.html#05000000 |title = 2018 Michigan Election Results |publisher = Michigan Department of State |date = September 27, 2018 |access-date = November 26, 2018 }}</ref>

On November 6, 2018, Stabenow defeated James, 52.3% to 45.8%.<ref name="2018General" />

===Potential United Nations ambassadorship===
In late November 2018, [[Bloomberg News]] reported that Trump was considering nominating James to become the [[United States Ambassador to the United Nations]], to replace Ambassador [[Nikki Haley]], who previously announced that she was planning to leave the Trump administration by the end of 2018. James reportedly met at the [[White House]] with Trump, [[Vice President of the United States|Vice President]] [[Mike Pence]], and [[United States Secretary of State|Secretary of State]] [[Mike Pompeo]].<ref>{{cite news |last=Jacobs |first=Jennifer |date=November 26, 2018 |title=Trump Considering Ex-Michigan Senate Candidate for UN Ambassador, Sources Say |url=https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2018-11-26/trump-is-said-to-consider-michigan-senate-candidate-for-un-post |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201208122333/https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2018-11-26/trump-is-said-to-consider-michigan-senate-candidate-for-un-post |archive-date=December 8, 2020 |access-date=May 10, 2026 |work=[[Bloomberg News]]}}</ref> He was ultimately bypassed for the position. Trump announced he would appoint [[Heather Nauert]], the [[spokesperson for the United States Department of State]] and a former television reporter, to succeed Haley,<ref>{{cite news |last1=Johnson |first1=Eliana |last2=Toosi |first2=Nahal |last3=Orr |first3=Gabby |last4=Strauss |first4=Daniel |name-list-style=amp |date=December 7, 2018 |title=Trump Picks State Department Spokeswoman for top UN Post |url=https://www.politico.com/story/2018/12/06/trump-next-un-ambassador-search-nikki-haley-1048194 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230216075129/https://www.politico.com/story/2018/12/06/trump-next-un-ambassador-search-nikki-haley-1048194 |archive-date=February 16, 2023 |access-date=May 10, 2026 |work=[[Politico]]}}</ref> but Nauert was never nominated and announced in February 2019 that she was withdrawing from consideration.<ref>{{cite news |last=Shesgreen |first=Deirdre |date=February 16, 2019 |title=Heather Nauert, President Trump's Choice for UN Ambassador Job, Withdraws in Surprise Move |url=https://www.freep.com/story/news/2019/02/16/president-trumps-choice-u-n-ambassador-heather-nauert-withdraws-needed-high-profile-position/2894857002/ |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20241203060325/https://www.freep.com/story/news/2019/02/16/president-trumps-choice-u-n-ambassador-heather-nauert-withdraws-needed-high-profile-position/2894857002/ |archive-date=December 3, 2024 |access-date=May 10, 2026 |work=[[USA Today]]}}</ref>

After Nauert's withdrawal, Trump again considered James for the ambassadorship,<ref>{{cite news |date=February 19, 2019 |title=Michigan's John James in Running for UN Ambassador, Reports Say |url=https://www.freep.com/story/news/politics/2019/02/19/john-james-united-nations-ambassador/2913257002/ |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230216075127/https://www.freep.com/story/news/politics/2019/02/19/john-james-united-nations-ambassador/2913257002/ |archive-date=February 16, 2023 |access-date=May 10, 2026 |work=[[Detroit Free Press]]}}</ref> but eventually nominated [[United States Ambassador to Canada|United States ambassador to Canada]] [[Kelly Knight Craft]] for the post.<ref>{{cite news |url = https://www.washingtonpost.com/politics/trump-announces-nomination-of-kelly-craft-as-us-ambassador-to-united-nations/2019/02/22/7ec4022a-36f6-11e9-854a-7a14d7fec96a_story.html |title = Trump Announces Nomination of Kelly Knight Craft to Be Ambassador to United Nations |newspaper = [[The Washington Post]] |first1 = Philip |last1 = Rucker |first2 = Anne |last2 = Gearan |name-list-style = amp |date = February 22, 2019 }}</ref>

===2020 U.S. Senate race===
{{Main|2020 United States Senate election in Michigan}}

Because the election margin in the 2018 Senate race was narrower than expected, James became a front-runner for the Republican nomination to take on Michigan's other incumbent Democratic senator, [[Gary Peters]], in the [[United States Senate election in Michigan, 2020|2020 election]].<ref>{{cite news |url = https://www.detroitnews.com/story/news/politics/2018/11/12/gop-bright-future-james-debbie-stabenow-race/1930266002/ |title = GOP Sees Bright Future for James After Close Senate Race |last = Fleming |first = Leonard N. |date = November 12, 2018 |work = The Detroit News |access-date = November 27, 2018 }}</ref><ref>{{cite news |first = Lauren |last = Gibbons |date = December 1, 2018 |url = https://www.mlive.com/news/index.ssf/2018/12/john_james_political_future.html |title = Michigan Republicans Hopeful in John James' Political Future After U.S. Senate Loss |website = [[MLive]] |publisher = Booth Michigan |language = en-US |access-date = January 11, 2019 }}</ref>

As well as being recruited to take on Peters, it was reported in June 2019 that the [[National Republican Congressional Committee]] was recruiting James to challenge freshman Democratic U.S. representative [[Haley Stevens]] of [[Michigan's 11th congressional district]].<ref>{{cite news |url = https://www.mlive.com/public-interest/2019/06/republicans-want-john-james-to-run-again-help-trump-win-michigan.html |title = Republicans Want John James to Run Again, Help Trump Win Michigan |last = Barrett |first = Malachi |date = June 5, 2019 |work = [[MLive]] |publisher = Booth Michigan |access-date = June 6, 2019 }}</ref>

On June 6, 2019, James announced that he was seeking the Republican nomination in [[2020 United States Senate election in Michigan|2020]] to take on Peters.<ref>{{cite web |url = https://www.detroitnews.com/story/news/politics/2019/06/05/john-james-site-senate-run/1357289001/ |title = John James Announces Second Run for U.S. Senate in Michigan |first1 = Melissa |last1 = Nann Burke |first2 = Jonathan |last2 = Oosting |name-list-style = amp |work = The Detroit News |date = June 5, 2019 |access-date = June 6, 2019 }}</ref> Michigan was one of two states in which an incumbent Democratic senator was seeking reelection during 2020 in a state won by Trump in [[2016 United States presidential election|2016]], the other being [[Alabama]].<ref>{{cite news |url = https://www.rollcall.com/news/10-vulnerable-senators-2020-republicans-play-defense |title = The Most Vulnerable Senators in 2020: Republicans Play Defense |first1 = Bridget |last1 = Bowman |first2 = Simone |last2 = Pathé |first3 = Stephanie |last3 = Akin |name-list-style = amp |date = November 4, 2019 |website = [[Roll Call]] |access-date = February 5, 2020 |archive-date = February 6, 2020 |archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20200206173326/https://www.rollcall.com/news/10-vulnerable-senators-2020-republicans-play-defense |url-status = dead }}</ref> Although the Associated Press called the race for Peters on November 4, 2020,<ref name="politicopeterswins" /> James refused to concede, which Peters termed "pathetic."<ref name=PetersSaysRefusal>{{Cite web |last = Eggert |first = David |date = November 5, 2020 |title = Peters Says James' Refusal to Concede Election 'Pathetic' |url = https://www.freep.com/story/news/local/michigan/2020/11/05/peters-says-james-refusal-concede-election-pathetic/6180450002/ |access-date = November 6, 2020 |website = [[Detroit Free Press]] |language = en-US }}</ref> James initially insisted that the election had not been administered fairly.<ref>{{Cite web |last = Gibbons |first = Lauren |date = November 5, 2020 |title = Attorney for John James says he has 'serious concerns' with Wayne County election process |url = https://www.mlive.com/politics/2020/11/attorney-for-john-james-says-he-has-serious-concerns-with-wayne-county-election-process.html |access-date = November 6, 2020 |website = [[MLive]] |publisher = Booth Michigan |language = en }}</ref> He established a joint legal fund with the [[Republican National Committee]] to challenge the results.<ref name="LegalFundContests">{{Cite web |date = November 11, 2020 |title = John James launches legal defense fund as he contests Michigan election results |url = https://www.fox2detroit.com/news/john-james-launches-legal-defense-fund-as-he-contests-michigan-election-results |publisher = WJBK }}</ref> James claimed there was "ample evidence" for an investigation, but offered none.<ref name="OffersNone">{{Cite web |last = Haddad |first = Ken |date = November 5, 2020 |title = With Peters ahead, John James says 'ample evidence' for investigation, but offers none |url = https://www.clickondetroit.com/news/local/2020/11/05/with-peters-ahead-john-james-says-ample-evidence-for-investigation-but-offers-none/ |publisher = WDIV-TV }}</ref> He raised $2 million after the election as he sought to challenge the election results, and he unsuccessfully attempted to block certification of the results of the election, which he lost to Peters by 1.7% of the vote, which was much closer than originally projected.<ref>{{cite news |first = Malachi |last = Barrett |title = John James raised $2M after election loss, collecting donations for challenging results |url = https://www.mlive.com/politics/2020/12/john-james-raised-2m-after-election-loss-collecting-donations-for-challenging-results.html |work = [[MLive]] |date = December 7, 2020 }}</ref> James conceded on November 24 over social media, congratulating Peters.<ref name="Concedes2020">{{Cite web |last = DesOrmeau |first = Taylor |date = November 25, 2020 |title = John James concedes Senate race, congratulates Gary Peters with bottle of Scotch |url = https://www.mlive.com/politics/2020/11/john-james-concedes-senate-race-congratulates-gary-peters-with-bottle-of-scotch.html |access-date = November 25, 2020 |website = [[MLive]] |language = en }}</ref>

During his campaign, James pledged to give 5% of his campaign contributions to charity. The James fundraising committee reported about $46.12 million in total contributions for the 2020 election and has given more than $2.36 million to charities following through on his pledge.<ref>{{cite web |title = John James campaign says it's achieved promise, giving $2.3M to charity |first = Craig |last = Mauger |url = https://www.detroitnews.com/story/news/politics/2021/07/16/james-campaign-says-its-achieved-vow-giving-2-3-m-charity/7990605002/ |website = The Detroit News |date = July 16, 2021 }}</ref>

== U.S. House of Representatives ==

===Elections===
====2022====
{{main|2022 United States House of Representatives elections in Michigan#District 10}}

James won the Republican primary in the 2022 election in [[Michigan's 10th congressional district]].<ref>{{cite news |last1 = Schnell |first1 = Mychael |title = Republican John James announces congressional bid in Michigan |url = https://thehill.com/homenews/campaign/592071-john-james-announces-congressional-bid-in-michigan |access-date = March 7, 2022 |work = [[The Hill (newspaper)|The Hill]] |date = January 31, 2022 |language = en }}</ref> He defeated Democrat [[Carl Marlinga]] in the November general election.<ref>{{cite news |title = John James, Carl Marlinga set to face off for 10th Congressional district |url = https://www.wxyz.com/news/john-james-carl-marlinga-set-to-face-off-for-10th-congressional-district |access-date = August 3, 2022 |publisher = [[WXYZ-TV]] |date = August 3, 2022 |language = en }}</ref>

====2024====
{{main|2024 United States House of Representatives elections in Michigan#District 10}}

James won a second term on November 5, 2024, in a rematch against Marlinga, winning 51.1% of the vote.<ref name="mich-2024-general-results">{{cite web |title = 2024 Michigan Election Results − General |url = https://mvic.sos.state.mi.us/votehistory/Index?type=C&electionDate=11-5-2024 |publisher = [[Michigan Secretary of State]] |access-date = November 24, 2024 |date = November 22, 2024 }}</ref>

===Committee assignments===

As of the [[118th United States Congress|118th Congress]], James is a member of the following committees.<ref>{{Cite web |title = Committees and Caucuses |date = January 3, 2023 |url = https://james.house.gov/about/committees-and-caucuses }}</ref>

*[[United States House Committee on Transportation and Infrastructure|Committee on Transportation and Infrastructure]]
**[[United States House Transportation Subcommittee on Water Resources and Environment|Subcommittee on Water Resources and Environment]] (vice chair)
**[[United States House Transportation Subcommittee on Aviation|Subcommittee on Aviation]]
*[[United States House Committee on Foreign Affairs|Committee on Foreign Affairs]]
**[[United States House Foreign Affairs Subcommittee on Africa|Subcommittee on Africa]] (Chairman)
**[[United States House Foreign Affairs Subcommittee on Western Hemisphere|Subcommittee for Western Hemisphere]]
*[[United States House Committee on Education and the Workforce|Committee on Education and the Workforce]]
**[[United States House Education Subcommittee on Higher Education and Workforce Development|Subcommittee on Higher Education and Workforce Development]]
*[[United States House Committee on Energy and Commerce|Committee on Energy and Commerce]]

==Political positions==
[[File:United States Congressional Delegation visit to Israel on November 12, 2023 - 20.jpg|thumb|James and other members of the US&nbsp;Congress with [[Benjamin Netanyahu]] in Israel, November 12, 2023]]
During his 2018 Senate campaign, James ran on a typical Republican platform, describing himself on his campaign website as "a pro-life, pro-[[Second Amendment to the United States Constitution|second amendment]], pro-business conservative."<ref name=":0">{{cite news |first = Malachi |last = Barrett |url = https://www.mlive.com/public-interest/2019/09/john-james-says-his-2020-senate-run-is-about-michigan-not-trump.html |title = John James Says His 2020 Senate Run Is About Michigan, not Trump |date = September 21, 2019 |website = [[MLive]] |publisher = Booth Michigan |language = en |access-date = April 18, 2020 |quote = Among James' top legislative priorities is creating an educational environment that gives parents and teachers more power, saying poor education outcomes are the root cause of many of the country’s problems. }}</ref> He emphasized his desire to defund [[Planned Parenthood]]<ref name=":0" /> and compared ''[[Roe v. Wade]]'', the [[Supreme Court of the United States|United States Supreme Court]] decision legalizing abortion, to "[[genocide]]."<ref>{{Cite news |first = Malachi |last = Barrett |url = https://www.mlive.com/public-interest/2019/11/michigan-democrats-hit-john-james-for-anti-abortion-comments.html |title = Michigan Democrats Hit John James for Anti-Abortion Comments |date = November 23, 2019 |website = [[MLive]] |publisher = Booth Michigan |language = en |access-date = April 23, 2020 }}</ref> He opposes the [[Capital punishment in the United States|death penalty]], does not believe employers should be able to fire workers due to their sexual orientation, and opposes the legalization of recreational marijuana.<ref name=":1" />

James says he wants to repeal and replace the [[Patient Protection and Affordable Care Act|Affordable Care Act]] (Obamacare), which he has called "a monstrosity."<ref>{{cite news |last = Perks |first = Ashley |date = December 3, 2019 |title = GOP Set for All-Out Battle over Michigan Senate Seat |language = en |website = [[The Hill (newspaper)|The Hill]] |url = https://thehill.com/homenews/campaign/472710-gop-set-for-all-out-battle-over-michigan-senate-seat |access-date = April 18, 2020 }}</ref> According to ''The Detroit Free Press'', James was careful not to take a position on the [[California v. Texas|Trump administration's lawsuit]] seeking to immediately strike down the entire ACA as unconstitutional.<ref name="Spangler" /> When pressed in a September 2020 interview, he said he was against the ACA lawsuit without a replacement plan in place, but did not criticize Republicans for pushing the lawsuit.<ref>{{cite news |first = Nick |last = LaFave |url = https://www.wzzm13.com/article/news/local/james-against-aca-lawsuit/69-e19a55e9-fe37-4f3b-96e0-347a8d050f22 |title = After Months, John James Says He Is Against ACA Lawsuit Without a 'Plan in Place' |location = Walker, Michigan |publisher = [[WZZM-TV]] |date = September 28, 2020 }}</ref>

James supported [[Ted Cruz]] in the [[2016 Republican Party presidential primaries]].<ref name=":1">{{Cite news |first1 = Leonard N. |last1 = Fleming |first2 = Jonathan |last2 = Oosting |date = July 6, 2018 |title = James, Pensler clash on abortion, Trump in GOP U.S. Senate debate |url = https://www.detroitnews.com/story/news/local/michigan/2018/07/06/james-pensler-clash-michigan-republican-senate-debate/760306002/ |website = The Detroit News |language = en |access-date = May 9, 2020 }}</ref> He later became a Trump supporter,<ref name=":1" /> and [[Twitter|tweeted]] in 2018 that, if elected to the Senate, he would back Trump "2,000%."<ref>{{cite news |url = https://www.detroitnews.com/story/news/politics/2019/06/05/john-james-site-senate-run/1357289001/ |title = John James Pursues Second Run for U.S. Senate in Michigan |last = Nann Burke |first = Melissa |date = June 5, 2019 |website = The Detroit News |language = en |access-date = April 18, 2020 }}</ref><ref>{{cite news |first = Todd |last = Spanger |url = https://www.freep.com/story/news/politics/elections/2018/10/14/debbie-stabenow-john-james-debate/1644399002/ |title = Stabenow, James Go After Each Other in First U.S. Senate Debate |work = [[Detroit Free Press]] |date = October 15, 2018 }}</ref> During his 2020 campaign, James accepted Trump's endorsement and campaigned alongside him.<ref>{{cite news |first = Malachi |last = Barrett |url = https://www.mlive.com/public-interest/2020/09/donald-trump-offers-total-and-complete-endorsement-of-john-james-in-michigan.html |title = Donald Trump Offers 'Total and Complete Endorsement' of John James in Michigan |work = [[MLive]] |publisher = Booth Michigan |date = September 10, 2020 }}</ref><ref>{{cite news |first = Melissa |last = Nann Burke |url = https://www.detroitnews.com/story/news/politics/2020/06/10/democrats-white-house-event-promoting-john-james-violated-hatch-act/5336743002/ |title = Democrats: White House Event Promoting John James Violated Hatch Act |work = The Detroit News |date = June 10, 2020 }}</ref> James has not been publicly critical of Trump or his actions.<ref name=Spangler/> During a meeting with black faith leaders, James was asked whether he disagreed with Trump on anything. James said, "Everything from cutting Great Lakes funding to '[[Racial views of Donald Trump#"Shithole countries"|shithole countries]]' to speaking ill of the dead. I mean, where do you want to start?"<ref>{{cite news |first = Alex |last = Isenstadt |url = https://www.politico.com/news/2020/05/09/republican-candidate-john-james-willing-to-call-out-donald-trump-244989 |title = The One Republican Senate Candidate Willing to Call Out Donald Trump |work = [[Politico]] |date = May 9, 2020 }}</ref> In a leaked audio recording of a meeting with African American leaders in Michigan, James was asked why he hadn't publicly criticized Trump. He said he thought it was better to be silent in public in order to gain access to Trump. James said, "Donald Trump doesn't need less Black folks around him, he needs more," and that his goal was "achieving equity and equality for our people, not standing up on Twitter and condemning folks."<ref name=DeVito>{{cite news |first = Lee |last = DeVito |url = https://www.metrotimes.com/news-hits/archives/2020/05/29/republican-senate-candidate-john-james-says-its-pointless-to-call-out-trumps-racism-in-leaked-audio |title = Republican Senate Candidate John James Says It's Pointless to Call Out Trump's Racism in Leaked Audio |work = Detroit Metro Times |date = May 29, 2020 |access-date = October 9, 2020 |archive-date = July 31, 2021 |archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20210731164705/https://www.metrotimes.com/news-hits/archives/2020/05/29/republican-senate-candidate-john-james-says-its-pointless-to-call-out-trumps-racism-in-leaked-audio |url-status = dead }}</ref> During the campaign, Democrats sought to tie James to Trump, while James has said his candidacy was not a referendum on Trump.<ref name=":0"/>

During his 2020 campaign, James declined to take specific positions on a number of policy questions, including how the [[Social Security Trust Fund]] would be protected from the impact of a [[Federal Insurance Contributions Act tax|payroll tax cut]], whether the Senate should vote to confirm a new [[Supreme Court of the United States|Supreme Court]] justice to fill the vacancy created by the [[Death and state funeral of Ruth Bader Ginsburg|death of Ruth Bader Ginsburg]] before or after the 2020 presidential election, and whether he thinks [[List of U.S. Army installations named for Confederate soldiers|military bases named for Confederate generals]] should be renamed.<ref name=Spangler>{{cite news |first = Todd |last = Spangler |url = https://www.freep.com/story/news/politics/elections/2020/09/24/john-james-gary-peters-senate-race/5863969002/ |title = In One of the Nastier Races, John James and Sen. Gary Peters Offer Different Styles |work = [[Detroit Free Press]] |date = September 24, 2020 }}</ref><ref>{{cite news |first = Malachi |last = Barrett |url = https://www.mlive.com/public-interest/2020/09/michigan-democrats-push-john-james-to-clarify-stance-on-supreme-court-vacancy.html |title = Michigan Democrats Push John James to Clarify Stance on Supreme Court Vacancy |work = [[MLive]] |publisher = Booth Michigan |date = September 24, 2020 }}</ref><ref>{{cite web |first = Clara |last = Hendrickson |url = https://www.freep.com/story/news/politics/2020/09/25/john-james-supreme-court-trump/3530574001/ |title = John James Stands Out for Not Taking Stance on Vacant Supreme Court Seat |work = [[Detroit Free Press]] |date = September 25, 2020 }}</ref>

In July 2026, James jointly penned a letter to Prime Minister Mark Carney demanding immediate action from the Canadian government regarding wildfire smoke originating from wildfires burning nearby in Canada. This letter included the passage "Sovereignty comes with responsibility, and the responsibility to prevent a foreseeable disaster from crossing into another country's airspace has not been met".<ref>{{Cite web |title='Sovereignty comes with responsibility:' U.S. lawmakers to Canada on wildfires - National |url=https://globalnews.ca/news/11968608/wildfires-united-states-canada/ |access-date=2026-07-17 |website=Global News |language=en-US}}</ref>

During a July 2026 [[Newsmax]] interview about [[Islamic extremism]], James said "I didn't go and fight extremists to have them on our doorstep here. Under no circumstances will we be a home in Michigan for extremism." The [[Council on American-Islamic Relations]] (CAIR) accused James of [[Islamophobia]] and James said he would seek to designate CAIR as a terrorist organization.<ref>{{Cite web|last=Hendrickson|first=Clara|date=July 7, 2026 |title=James responds to Islamophobia accusation, vowing to target Muslim org|url=https://www.freep.com/story/news/politics/2026/07/07/john-james-accused-of-islamophobia-in-dearborn-comments/90821549007/?gnt-cfr=1&gca-cat=p&gca-uir=true&gca-epti=z11xx58p116250l116450c116250e1198xxv11xx58d--48--b--48--&gca-ft=229&gca-ds=sophi |access-date=2026-07-26 |website=Detroit Free Press |language=en-US}}</ref> James said "Whether you are Christian, Jewish, Muslim or Hindu, I will fiercely defend your religious freedom and every Michigan resident's right to worship peacefully — or not worship at all."<ref>{{cite news |title=John James escalates clash with Michigan Muslims, targets CAIR |url=https://arabamericannews.com/2026/07/10/john-james-doubles-down-on-ashura-remarks-vows-to-label-cair-a-terrorist-organization-if-elected-governor/ |access-date=28 August 2026 |work=ArabAmericanNews |date=10 July 2026}}</ref>

==2026 gubernatorial campaign==
{{main|2026 Michigan gubernatorial election}}

On April 7, 2025, James announced his candidacy for [[governor of Michigan]] in the [[2026 Michigan gubernatorial election|2026 election]].<ref name="announce">{{Cite web |last = Powers |first = Sara |date = April 7, 2025 |title = Rep. John James announces run for Michigan governor |url = https://www.clickondetroit.com/news/local/2025/04/07/rep-john-james-announces-run-for-michigan-governor/ |access-date = April 7, 2025 |website = ClickOnDetroit |location = Detroit |publisher = WDIV-TV |language = en }}</ref> James won the primary and will face Democratic nominee [[Jocelyn Benson]] in the general election.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Gomez |first1=Henry |title=Trump-backed John James wins Michigan’s GOP primary for governor |url=https://www.nbcnews.com/politics/2026-election/trump-backed-john-james-wins-michigans-gop-primary-governor-rcna590045 |access-date=28 August 2026 |work=NBC News |date=5 August 2026 |language=en}}</ref>

If elected, James would become Michigan's first African-American governor.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2026/08/04/us/elections/michigan-republican-governor-primary.html|title=Trump-Backed Lawmaker Wins Republican Nomination for Michigan Governor|first=Mitch|last=Smith|date=August 5, 2026|via=NYTimes.com}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.yahoo.com/news/politics/articles/john-james-wins-gop-primary-152000394.html|title=John James wins GOP primary, seeks to become Michigan’s first Black governor|date=August 6, 2026|website=Yahoo News}}</ref>

==Personal life==
James married his wife, Elizabeth, in 2012.<ref>{{cite tweet |first = John |last = James |user = johnjamesmi |number = 946720226791575553 |date = December 29, 2017 |title = Five years ago today, I married the love of my life. And you have said 'I do' to our crazy adventure together 1,825 times since! Happy Anniversary, Elizabeth. Your beauty is only surpassed by your mettle. You make me feel like we can change the world! |language = English }}</ref> They have three sons.<ref>{{cite web |last1 = Krafcik |first1 = Michael |title = U.S. Senate: John James, Republican |url = https://wwmt.com/news/on-the-ballot/us-senate-john-james-republican |location = Kalamazoo, Michigan |publisher = [[WWMT-TV]] |access-date = October 9, 2020 |date = October 5, 2020 }}</ref> When James was still dating Elizabeth, he had an encounter with police at a mall in a suburb of Detroit in which the officers drew their guns on him; James believes that if Elizabeth had not been beside him, he might have been killed. He has also expressed his fear of being killed whenever police pull him over for a [[traffic stop]].<ref>{{cite news |last1 = Peters |first1 = Jeremy W. |last2 = Gray |first2 = Kathleen |name-list-style = amp |title = A Black Republican Feels the Sting of Racism but Is Silent on Trump |url = https://www.nytimes.com/2020/07/08/us/politics/john-james-michigan-senate.html |access-date = October 9, 2020 |work = [[The New York Times]] |date = July 29, 2020 }}</ref>

James is a [[Nondenominational Christianity|nondenominational Christian]].<ref name=":4" /> He lives in [[Shelby Charter Township, Michigan|Shelby Township, Michigan]].<ref name=MLive>{{Cite web |title = John James Claims Victory in Michigan's 10th Congressional District |url = https://www.mlive.com/politics/2024/11/john-james-claims-victory-in-michigans-10th-congressional-district.html |access-date = November 10, 2024 |website = MLive |date = November 6, 2024 |language = en }}</ref>

